                    rdf:value "data are selected from the ﬁlt_global preprocessing stratagem, which is band-pass ﬁltered (0.01–0.1 Hz) and spatially registered using a nonlinear method to MNI152 template space" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3389/fninf.2021.635657> ;
            slr:claim_label "Specific CCS preprocessing strategy used (filt_global)." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.6"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "improving the performance of a simple k-nn classiﬁer by 11.9% compared to a traditional distance metric" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-66182-7_54> ;
            slr:claim_label "The learned GCN distance metric improves k-nn ASD-vs-control classification accuracy by 11.9 percentage points over Euclidean/PCA distance." ;
            slr:relevance_score "1.0"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The implemented code is available on GitHub portal of our lab at: https:// github.com/pcdslab/ASD-SAENet." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3389/fncom.2021.654315> ;
            slr:claim_label "Classifier code released on GitHub." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.6"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value """The Automated Anatomical Labelling (AAL) atlas, with 116 ROIs, is widely
used in ASD diagnosis studies [16, 35, 36, 37] due to its ability to provide precise anatomical lo-
cations essential for identifying structural abnormalities caused by ASD. The Craddock 200 atlas
[38], with 200 ROIs, is based on functional connectivity and is particularly useful for analysing
resting-state fMRI data [39, 9, 36, 37], as it clusters the brain into functionally homogeneous
regions.
Recent studies have leveraged multiple atlases to capture diverse patterns related to ASD.
For instance, Mahler et al. [37] proposed a multi-atlas framework for ASD classification using
resting-state fMRI data, employing three atlases, including AAL and Craddock 200. Similarly,
Deng et al. [9] utilised the AAL, Craddock 200 and Craddock 400 atlases. Their experiment in-
dicates that Craddock 200 can outperform Craddock 400 in fMRI-based ASD diagnosis. Hence,
we selected the Craddock 200 atlas, hereinafter referred to as the CC atlas.
To further enhance the analysis, we leverage the Eickhoff-Zilles (EZ) atlas with 116 ROIs,
which has also been utilised in recent ASD diagnosis studies [40, 41]. Derived from cytoarchi-
tectonic mapping, the EZ atlas combines anatomical and functional data, offering a nuanced view
that bridges structural and functional aspects of brain regions""" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1016/j.compbiomed.2024.109083> ;
            slr:claim_label "Three atlases were used: AAL (116 ROIs), Craddock 200 (CC), and Eickhoff-Zilles (EZ)." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.8"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "We minimized the risk of overﬁtting by using three limiting approaches. First, the simplest kernel (linear) was used for the SVM. Second, only 10 features were used to learn to classify between 104 subjects. Finally, using 10-fold cross validation ensured the model is only evaluated on data points that it has not experienced before." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3389/fnins.2018.01018> ;
            slr:claim_label "Overfitting was limited using a linear SVM kernel, only 10 selected features, and 10-fold cross-validation so the model was evaluated only on unseen data." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value """It
 consists of 1, 112 subjects, including 539 from individuals with ASD""" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1109/tmi.2023.3337362> ;
            slr:claim_label "Full ABIDE I release referenced: 1,112 subjects, 539 ASD." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.6"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "ABIDE dataset [4,7] consists of resting state fMRI brain scans from 16 diﬀerent imaging sites." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-32248-9_82> ;
            slr:claim_label "Cohort was ABIDE I, 16 imaging sites." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.7"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value """ABIDE I dataset consists of 1112 
total samples including 539 patients and 573 typical 
controls (TC) subjects. While ABIDE II dataset consists 
of 1114 total samples with 521 patients and 573 
typical controls (TC) subjects.""" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.11113/jurnalteknologi.v83.16389> ;
            slr:claim_label "The study used the full ABIDE I (1112) and ABIDE II (1114) releases, 539/573 and 521/573 ASD/TD respectively." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "After randomly perform- ing fivefold cross-validation data division, in each round of experiments, one-fold data    Page 11 of 20 Hu et al. BMC Bioinformatics          (2021) 22:379  	 were used for testing while other fourfold data were used for t" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1186/s12859-021-04295-1> ;
            slr:claim_label "Evaluation used random fivefold cross-validation (no leave-site-out)." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.7"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "We used the CC400 functional parcellation atlas of the brain throughout our study." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3389/fnins.2019.01325> ;
            slr:claim_label "The CC400 functional parcellation atlas was used throughout." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.8"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value """Both anatomical and functional atlases were chosen to derive the 
FC measures; in particular, we chose Harvard-Oxford (HO) and 
Automatic Anatomical Labeling (AAL) as anatomical templates 
and Craddock-200 (CC) as functional templates to extract time 
series from brain regions""" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3389/fpsyt.2019.00620> ;
            slr:claim_label "Four atlases (AAL, HO, CC, Power) were compared for the connectivity analysis." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.8"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "We present a site-wise de-meaning (SWD) strategy in multi-site FC analysis and compare its performance with two common site-effect mitigation methods, i.e., generalized linear model (GLM) and Combining Batches (ComBat) Harmonization." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3389/fncom.2021.762781> ;
            slr:claim_label "Main method: site-wise de-meaning (SWD) compared against GLM and ComBat for multi-site FC harmonisation." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.8"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Firstly, the 84 samples are divided into 58 training samples and 26 test samples based on the ratio of 7:3. Then 50 samples are randomly selected from the 58 training samples and 62 features are randomly selected from 4,225 features, which form a single SVM using Radial Basis Function (RBF) as kernel provided by the SVM toolbox (http://see.xidian.edu.cn/faculty/chzheng/ bishe/indexﬁles/indexl.htm) (Gunn, 1998). The width argument of RBF equals to 3, and penalty coeﬃcient equals to Inf. The process is repeated for 500 times and these 500 SVMs constitute into a random SVM cluster." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3389/fgene.2018.00018> ;
            slr:claim_label "The 84 samples were split once into 58 training and 26 test samples (ratio 7:3) to build and evaluate the random SVM cluster." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "We showcase our approach on a challenging large-scale dataset (ABIDE, with N > 2, 000) and report state-of-the-art accuracy results on rs-fMRI- based discrimination of autism patients and healthy controls." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-030-00889-5_16> ;
            slr:claim_label "The study classifies autism vs. control using ABIDE resting-state fMRI connectomes with a 3D CNN." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Data were harmo­ nized for site effects while preserving effects of age and  ASD diagnosis using ComBat [86]" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1186/s13229-024-00616-2> ;
            slr:claim_label "Site effects were handled using ComBat harmonization." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.7"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value """(1) FC val-
ues significantly different [family-wise error (FWE)-corrected p < 0.05] between the ASD and TD groups and (2) 
voxels inside the corresponding R-masks to create A1–A20 masks (ASD > TD: A1 to A10 and ASD < TD: A11 to 
A20).""" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1038/s41598-019-45465-9> ;
            slr:claim_label "Biomarker masks (A1-A20) were derived from FWE-corrected two-sample t-test group differences between ASD and TD in ABIDE, with explicit direction (A1-A10: ASD>TD; A11-A20: ASD<TD)." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value """Availability of data and materials
The datasets analysed during the current study are available from a world-wide multi-site database Autism Brain Imaging 
Data Exchange (ABIDE I) (http://​prepr​ocess​ed-​conne​ctomes-​proje​ct.​org/).""" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1186/s12859-023-05495-7> ;
            slr:claim_label "Data-availability statement points only to the general ABIDE/Preprocessed Connectomes Project resource, not a study-specific participant list or code." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.5"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "elastic deformations seem to work best, with an improvement of 2.2 percentage units." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3390/diagnostics13172773> ;
            slr:claim_label "With a fixed number of epochs, elastic deformation augmentation gave the largest improvement (2.2 percentage points)." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.8"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value """Each subject was scanned at
one of the following seven diﬀerent sites: California Institute of
Technology (Caltech), Carnegie Mellon University (CMU), NYU
Langone Medical Center (NYU), University of Pittsburgh School
of Medicine (Pitt), San Diego State University (SDSU), Trinity
Center for Health Sciences (Trinity), and University of California
Los Angeles (UCLA).""" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3389/fams.2018.00025> ;
            slr:claim_label "ABIDE cohort was drawn from seven sites." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.6"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value """The mean time series from 116 regions of interest (ROIs)
based on the automated anatomical labeling (AAL) atlas [23] are used for experiments.""" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1109/tnsre.2022.3233656> ;
            slr:claim_label "Functional connectivity features used the AAL atlas (116 ROIs)." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.7"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value """For the intra-site cross validation, the 
highest accuracy resulted in 71.2%, and the mean classification 
accuracy was 67.7% (sensitivity=66.3%, specificity=68.9%) with a 
range of accuracy of 64.0% to 71.2% in individual folds.""" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.5607/en.2020.29.1.27> ;
            slr:claim_label "Intra-site cross-validation gave a mean accuracy of 67.7% (range 64.0-71.2% across folds), somewhat lower than the pooled 10-fold result." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.8"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value """The samples were separated into two folders 
which were the training file and test file.""" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.11113/jurnalteknologi.v83.16389> ;
            slr:claim_label "Evaluation used a single train/test split with no cross-validation scheme described." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.6"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value """For ADHD and AD, highest similarity
was achieved between connectivity and phenotypic clusters, whereas for ASD
and PTSD/PCS, highest similarity was achieved between connectivity and clinical
diagnostic clusters.""" ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3389/fams.2018.00025> ;
            slr:claim_label "For ASD, connectivity-based clusters corresponded most closely to clinical diagnostic groups (rather than to phenotypic clusters)." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.95"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "Since no participants in the NYU_2 dataset had complete cerebellar coverage, this dataset was excluded. In addition, participants with excessive maximum head motion (>3 mm) or failure of normalization were excluded." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3389/fnhum.2018.00454> ;
            slr:claim_label "Additional exclusion rules (imaging coverage, motion >3mm, normalization failure) applied to the ABIDE II subset." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.9"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "The model achieved the following performance metrics, presented in mean ± standard deviation format, on the ABIDE A dataset: accuracy (ACC) of 87.38 ± 2.87%, area under curve (AUC) of 92.76 ± 4.00%, speciﬁcity (SEN) of 88.35 ± 6.83%, and sensitivity (SPE) of 86.51 ± 8.41%." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.1038/s41398-024-02972-2> ;
            slr:claim_label "Main ABIDE A result: accuracy 87.38%, AUC 92.76%." ;
            slr:relevance_score "1.0"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value "we downloaded fMRI data (derivatives) pro- cessed with the Connectome Computation System (CCS) pipeline [9] which performs slice timing correction, motion realignment and global intensity normalisation." ] ;
            oa:hasTarget <https://doi.org/10.3390/diagnostics13172773> ;
            slr:claim_label "fMRI derivatives processed with the CCS (Connectome Computation System) pipeline." ;
            slr:relevance_score "0.8"^^xsd:float ],
        [ a oa:Annotation ;
            oa:hasBody [ a dcterms:Text ;
                    rdf:value """we used functional magnetic resonance imaging (fMRI) of 708 subjects under a resting
