                    slr:domain_name "Exposure measured validly (rs-fMRI pipeline)" ;
                    slr:judgment "low" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "we performed preprocessing based on the Conﬁgurable Pipeline for the Analysis of Connectomes (C-PAC, http://fcp-indi.github.io). The images were slice-time and motion corrected." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Appropriate statistical analysis (multiple comparisons)" ;
                    slr:judgment "high" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "We further validate the detected subnetwork features by performing classiﬁcation analysis. We employ the support vector machine with radial basis function kernel and linear kernel as our classiﬁer. The classification features (detected abnormal subnetworks) were identified using the full case-control sample before being validated with leave-one-out cross-validation, risking optimistic performance estimates from feature-selection leakage." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Confounders identified (age, sex, site, motion)" ;
                    slr:judgment "low" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "Age and sex compared between groups (Wilcoxon, chi-square) with no significant difference; single-site design removes between-site variance but limits generalisability." ],
                [ a slr:RoBDomainJudgment ;
                    slr:domain_name "Subjects and setting described" ;
                    slr:judgment "low" ;
                    slr:supporting_quote "The data set was collected at the University of Michigan, one of the ABIDE data collection sites Age, sex and IQ comparisons reported with no significant group differences." ] ;
            slr:overall_judgment "high" ;
            slr:tool_used "JBI Critical Appraisal" ] .

<https://doi.org/10.3389/fnins.2018.01018> a fabio:Expression,
        slr:IncludedSource ;
    dcterms:abstract "Automatic algorithms for disease diagnosis are being thoroughly researched for use in clinical settings. They usually rely on pre-identified biomarkers to highlight the existence of certain problems. However, finding such biomarkers for neurodevelopmental disorders such as Autism Spectrum Disorder (ASD) has challenged researchers for many years. With enough data and computational power, machine learning (ML) algorithms can be used to interpret the data and extract the best biomarkers from thousands of candidates. In this study, we used the fMRI data of 816 individuals enrolled in the Autism Brain Imaging Data Exchange (ABIDE) to introduce a new biomarker extraction pipeline for ASD that relies on the use of graph theoretical metrics of fMRI-based functional connectivity to inform a support vector machine (SVM). Furthermore, we split the dataset into 5 age groups to account for the effect of aging on functional connectivity. Our methodology achieved better results than most state-of-the-art investigations on this dataset with the best model for the >30 years age group achieving an accuracy, sensitivity, and specificity of 95, 97, and 95%, respectively. Our results suggest that measures of centrality provide the highest contribution to the classification power of the models." ;
    dcterms:bibliographicCitation "Amirali Kazeminejad; Roberto C. Sotero (2019). Topological Properties of Resting-State fMRI Functional Networks Improve Machine Learning-Based Autism Classification. *Frontiers in Neuroscience*. https://doi.org/10.3389/fnins.2018.01018" ;
    dcterms:creator "Amirali Kazeminejad",
        "Roberto C. Sotero" ;
    dcterms:title "Topological Properties of Resting-State fMRI Functional Networks Improve Machine Learning-Based Autism Classification" ;
    bibo:doi "10.3389/fnins.2018.01018" ;
    bibo:pmid "local_bb07b369" ;
    fabio:hasPublicationYear 2019 ;
